---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Examples" current="/guides/examples/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
      <h1>Examples</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The repository includes local-schema, nested-config, SchemaStore, and
        Azure ARM examples so you can test the main workflows without inventing
        fixtures from scratch.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Local schemas</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./examples/basics --locations --show-skipped</code></pre>
    <p>
      The basics fixture includes valid and invalid JSON, YAML, and TOML files,
      plus one file without a schema to demonstrate skipped-file accounting.
      JSONC, JSON5, and JSON Lines use the same validation flow when included in
      your own fixtures.
    </p>

    <h2>Nested configs</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./examples/nested-configs --locations</code></pre>
    <p>
      This fixture enables <code>configs.mode = "nearest"</code>. The root config
      validates root-level JSON files, while <code>packages/api/.dollarlint.toml</code>
      extends the root config and overrides the schema association for its
      subtree.
    </p>

    <h2>SchemaStore-backed files</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./examples/schemastore --schema-store --locations</code></pre>
    <p>
      These examples cover common config files such as <code>package.json</code
      >, <code>tsconfig.json</code>, <code>renovate.json</code>, <code
        >buf.yaml</code
      >, and <code>ruff.toml</code>. Some declare schemas directly; catalog
      matching can infer schemas for conventional filenames.
    </p>

    <h2>Azure ARM templates</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./examples/azure --locations</code></pre>
    <p>
      The Azure examples use real <code
        >https://schema.management.azure.com</code
      > schema URLs and exercise the Azure resource pruning optimization. The directory
      includes one intentionally invalid template. The default output reports the
      closest matching schema branch for that invalid storage account; set <code
        >output.branchErrors = "all"</code
      > if you want to inspect every failed Azure resource branch.
    </p>

    <h2>Machine-readable output</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./examples/basics --format json
dollarlint validate ./examples/basics --format sarif --output dollarlint.sarif</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use JSON when a script needs run summaries, relative paths, issue
      categories, ignored issue details, and line/column metadata. Use SARIF when
      CI should upload code scanning results.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
